********************************************************************************

                     AN ANALYSIS OF MONITOR MULTITASK BEHAVIOR

********************************************************************************

The existence of monitors in IP Pascal not only gives the language a construct
for parallel tasking, but also gives it the ability to control the interactive
consequences of tasking. For example, the existance of lock and signal calls
as compiler constructs give the compiler the ability to clear register
caches of variables before such a construct.

THE LOCKING METHOD

Each monitor is is own "domain" for a lock. The rules are:

1. Only one lock can be held at a time.

2. At all times when executing monitor code, the lock for that monitor is held.

3. When leaving a monitor for any reason, the monitor lock is held, and 
reaquired after the call.

This set of perhaps draconian rules effectively prevents deadlock, since there
is no way for a monitor to "travel" while holding a lock. The deadlock case 
really becomes a infinite loop case, since the only way to deadlock becomes:

monitor deadlock;

procedure stop; forward;

private

procedure stop;

begin

   while true do

end;

begin
end.

An important corolary to the single lock rule is that any var reference data
exported from a monitor is at risk, since the monitor that owns the data will
have to drop its lock to call another monitor. This would also create a risk
for var references imported from tasks, then exported again by that monitor.

So the rule becomes:

No monitor can call another monitor using a var reference parameter

This rule is enforceable by the parser.

Calling the monitor with a var reference is ok. This effectively allows a 
process to place its data on trust with a monitor routine, provided that the
monitor promises not to give the data out to other monitors.

IMPLEMENTATION

Since all locks, signals and tasks are tracked in syslib, it can coordinate
the interactions between modules. Syslib can keep track of what lock a monitor
holds, and when a monitor requests a lock, meaning it may be traversing to a new
monitor, the previous lock is dropped, and the new lock is aquired. syslib does
not have to keep track of what monitor is being executed, because it keeps a
pushdown list of the locks held.

This would tend to argue that a module should unlock before performing external
calls, and relock after. However, there is already a reason why locks need be
"overlapped", the signal case, discussed later. Also you can't know what might
be needed to solve special problems. So even though it would help speed, we let
the lock and unlock calls perform their own crossover management.

In effect, this is what it means:

When a call is made from a monitor to an external routine, it is either to
a monitor or a share. If it is a share, the lock is held, since the share is
acting completely as the monitors agent.

If it is a monitor, it is either the same monitor or a different one. For a new
monitor, the monitor lock held is dropped, and the new one obtained. On return,
the new lock is dropped, and the old one renewed. Since we don't directly know
where we are monitor wise, this means we maintain the monitor lock stack to
track this.

If the monitor happens to be the same, either it can drop and renew the same 
lock, or detect that the same lock is being crossed, and simply track it without
any action.

